WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:02.960
INTRO:
We all know the founders of the biggest companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:09.280
and services in the world. Apple is Steve Jobs&nbsp;
and Steve Wozniak. Microsoft is Bill Gates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:15.120
Paul Allen. Google is Larry Page and Sergey&nbsp;
Brin, and so on and so forth. We also know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.000
electricity was discovered by Benjamin Franklin&nbsp;
and the telephone was invented by Alexander Graham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Bell and light bubl was invented by Thomas Edison&nbsp;
and Nikola Tesla. Yet, virtually none of us know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.120
the inventors of the network that we spend the&nbsp;
vast majority of our time on: the internet. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.080
internet was created by two computer scientists&nbsp;
named Vinton Cerf and Bob Kahn, and the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:42.080
was launched on January 1, 1983. Since then, the&nbsp;
internet has become an integral part of our lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.920
yet this duo doesn’t dominate the billionaires&nbsp;
list or Forbes magazines and interviews. No one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.720
would probably even recognize them on the street&nbsp;
which is ironic given how influential they are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.840
So, here’s how Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn created&nbsp;
the internet and what they’re up to today.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.560
VINT CERF:&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.640
Taking a look back, Vint Cerf was born on June&nbsp;
23, 1943 in New Haven, Connecticut. His father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.280
worked at an aeronautics company which gave Vint&nbsp;
an interest in engineering from very early on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.520
In fact, when he was in high school, he would get&nbsp;
a job at Rocketdyne where he got an opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.320
work on the Apollo program. He wrote statistical&nbsp;
analysis software that helped test the F-1 engines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.520
that was used by famous Saturn V. So, by the time&nbsp;
Vint graduated high school, he was already more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.800
accomplished than most of us ever will be, but&nbsp;
he was just getting started. Vint would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.080
to attend Stanford University where he majored in&nbsp;
mathematics. He would complete this degree in 1965&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.200
and go on to join IBM as a systems engineer. He&nbsp;
didn’t stick around here for long though as his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.720
true passion was research. So, he would return&nbsp;
to school to further his education. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:54.080
Vint went to UCLA where he pursued a masters in&nbsp;
computer science. This experience was especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.960
important because this is where he would meet the&nbsp;
other inventor of the internet: Bob Kahn. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.560
Vint would finish his masters in 1970 and he would&nbsp;
pursue a PhD in computer science also from UCLA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.320
It doesn’t look like Vint really wanted&nbsp;
to leave this lifestyle because he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.760
just become an assistant professor at Stanford&nbsp;
after graduating in 1972. For the next 4 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.520
he would spend the vast majority of this time&nbsp;
on researching packet network interconnection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:23.440
protocols and creating the TCP/IP protocol&nbsp;
suite. He would eventually leave academia in 1976&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.840
at age 33, but his new job wasn’t&nbsp;
all that different. He would join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.640
DARPA where he continued the same research&nbsp;
for the next 6 years. But unlike academia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.600
Vint had the opportunity to implement his&nbsp;
research into real projects at DARPA. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.320
the Department of Defense would use the 4th&nbsp;
version of TCP/IP for all military computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.760
networking purposes. Vint ended up working for&nbsp;
DARPA up until 1982 at which point he switched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.720
over to MCI Digital Information Services and this&nbsp;
is when the internet would officially go live.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:57.120
BOB KAHN:&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.360
Moving on Bob Kahn’s back story, Bob was born on&nbsp;
December 23, 1938 in New York. Like many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.760
people that we cover on this channel, Bob was&nbsp;
quite curious and intelligent from a very young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.160
age. Despite this, Bob was never able to fully&nbsp;
explore his interests throughout his childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.280
due to his family’s situation. You see, his mom&nbsp;
was suffering from an undisclosed heart condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.680
which meant that Bob had to spend much of his&nbsp;
freetime caring for his mother. In fact, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.080
one of the main reasons that he would attend local&nbsp;
colleges for his bachelors. First, he attended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.680
Queens college for two years before he transferred&nbsp;
over to the City College of New York where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.600
earned his bachelors in electrical engineering.&nbsp;
By the time he completed his bachelors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.960
Bob says that it was clear to him that he was not&nbsp;
a big fan lab work, but he did find math quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:45.120
intriguing. But ironically, in January of 1960,&nbsp;
Bob would get a job at Bell Labs which literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.520
has lab in the name. He didn’t stick around for&nbsp;
very long though. He was applying to master’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:54.720
programs and would be accepted into Princeton.&nbsp;
Princeton was a big jump up from City College,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.840
but Bob was up for the challenge. In fact, he&nbsp;
thrived under the pressure and would even end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.000
up running the graduate school’s seminar series.&nbsp;
Bob would end up getting his masters in electrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.360
engineering in 1962 and he would instantly turn&nbsp;
around and pursue a PhD in electrical engineering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.640
After completing his PhD in 1964, like Vint,&nbsp;
Bob would become an assistant professor but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.760
he would teach at MIT instead of Stanford. Bob&nbsp;
wasn’t a big fan of being a professor though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.840
He felt that the job “a little stultifying”&nbsp;
which means that it stifled enthusiasm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.320
initiative, and freedom of action. So, he only&nbsp;
did this for two years though before he switched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.680
over to an engineering consulting firm called BB&amp;N&nbsp;
where he served as senior scientist. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.200
BB&amp;N was working on a new computer network called&nbsp;
ARPANET which was being funded by DARPA. Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.920
didn’t know it at the time, but this is what would&nbsp;
eventually lead to the creation of the internet.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.000
CREATING THE INTERNET:&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.480
The characteristic that made ARPANET unique&nbsp;
was that it relied on a packet switching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.800
This is when a large piece of data is split up&nbsp;
into smaller chucks called packets which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.960
then sent over the network. These packets are&nbsp;
then combined to create the original piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:06.240
data at the destination. The researchers found&nbsp;
that this was far more efficient and reliable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.200
and packet switching has since become a standard&nbsp;
for transferring data over networks. Bob’s role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.600
in this project was to link up host computers&nbsp;
from institutions to this network. And as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.400
might’ve guessed, one of the institutions that&nbsp;
were involved in this project was UCLA. Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.760
would travel to UCLA in September of 1969 to setup&nbsp;
Arpanet Nodes at the university which led him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.840
meeting Vint Cerf. Anyway, a few years later in&nbsp;
1972, Bob would help organize the world’s first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:36.000
International Conference on Computer Communication&nbsp;
which is when ARPANET would be revealed to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.760
public. After the public reveal, Bob would&nbsp;
leave BB&amp;N and join DARPA full-time. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.480
he was tasked with linking together military radio&nbsp;
networks from around the world. In other words, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:51.760
had to create a network of networks hence the name&nbsp;
internet. Bob would recruit help from Stanford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.800
professor Vint Cerf and together they would work&nbsp;
on creating this network of networks. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:02.000
actually extremely challenging because the duo had&nbsp;
to connect more networks than they ever imagined.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.960
When Bob was given the job, he thought he had to&nbsp;
figure out how to connect 5 to 10 big networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.360
but he actually had to connect hundreds of&nbsp;
thousands which eventually turned into millions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.600
Using ARPANET didn’t seem feasible for&nbsp;
such a large task especially if they wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.320
it to last a long time, so the duo started&nbsp;
working on their own protocol called TCP/IP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.080
TCP stands for Transimission Control Protocol&nbsp;
and it’s responsible for splitting up the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.440
at the source and reassembling the&nbsp;
packets of data at the destination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.840
Meanwhile, IP stands for Internet Protocol and&nbsp;
it’s responsible for ensuring that the packets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.000
of data are actually sent to the right&nbsp;
destination. Over the next several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.080
Bob and Vint would perfect data transfers using&nbsp;
packet switching and they would eventually launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.840
TCP/IP guidelines in 1980. TCP/IP wasn’t the only&nbsp;
protocol that trying to link up these networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:53.440
Some other famous protocols include FTP or&nbsp;
File Transfer Protocol and NCP or Network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.360
Control Protocol. But, in the end, it was TCP/IP&nbsp;
that would become the standard for ARPANET. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:05.520
switch to TCP/IP took place on January 1, 1983 and&nbsp;
this was marked as the day the internet was born.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:09.840
ADOPTION:&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.320
While the internet was officially live, there was&nbsp;
still a long way to go in making this technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:19.760
accessible to the public and that’s exactly what&nbsp;
this duo would start working on next. By the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.640
ARPANET adopted the TCP/IP protocol, Vint had&nbsp;
already left DARPA and was working at MCI Digital.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.120
There, Vint would begin working on the world’s&nbsp;
first commercial email service that would work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.240
through the internet. Meanwhile, Bob would&nbsp;
stick around at DARPA a little longer. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.400
he too would leave in 1985 to found the&nbsp;
Corporation for National Research Initiatives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.720
This was a non-profit group that was dedicated&nbsp;
to making the internet accessible to the public.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.160
They accomplished this by sourcing funding for&nbsp;
projects that made the internet user friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.760
through tools like browsers. Vint would soon&nbsp;
join this organization as vice president in 1986&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.160
and help with the fundraising effort. The Reagan&nbsp;
administration was rather supportive of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.720
initiative, but the real money ended up coming&nbsp;
from the private sector as opposed to government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:08.720
By 1990, the private sector had donated $500&nbsp;
million. And these funds would eventually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.720
used to launch the world’s first internet&nbsp;
browser and ATM in 1990 with the help of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.960
AT&amp;T and loads of other programmers and&nbsp;
the rest is history. Moving on, in 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.560
Bob and Vint would create the Internet Society&nbsp;
together which was yet another non-profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.120
dedicated to promoting development and adoption&nbsp;
of the internet. Despite founding something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.440
was so influential neither Bob nor Vint really&nbsp;
made that much money from founding the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.120
itself. You see, no one really runs the internet.&nbsp;
It’s more of a decentralized network of networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.080
thats run by thousands of companies, universities,&nbsp;
and governments from around the world. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:46.800
there was really no way to monetize the internet&nbsp;
itself which is quite ironic given that dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.920
multi hundred billion dollar companies were built&nbsp;
off the internet. At one point, in 1988, Vint did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:57.040
try to privatize the internet, but the internet&nbsp;
was already too decentralized by this point, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.240
his efforts weren’t that successful. Fortunately&nbsp;
though, Vint would end up joining Google in 2005&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.840
as a vice president and this made him quite&nbsp;
wealthy. Today, he’s worth roughly $45 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.040
Bob, however, would just stick around at his&nbsp;
non-profits and he still works there to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.280
There’s no verifiable net worth for Bob, but&nbsp;
some sources put it at around $8 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.960
Obviously neither of these guys are struggling&nbsp;
for money, but given that people like Jeff Bezos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:28.720
Mark Zuckerberg, Larry Page, and Sergey Brin are&nbsp;
all worth $100 billion each, this is nothing.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.880
VINT &amp; BOB TODAY:&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.280
While Vint nor Bob never got the financial&nbsp;
benefits that they deserved, they did get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.000
plenty of awards. These guys have won so many&nbsp;
awards that if we went through them all, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:48.560
would likely take an hour, so we’ll just stick to&nbsp;
the two most notable awards. In December of 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.720
Bob and Vint were awarded the National Medal&nbsp;
of Technology from President Bill Clinton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:57.840
And several years later in 2005, both Vint and Bob&nbsp;
would be awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.960
from President Bush. I think we can all agree&nbsp;
that these two more than deserved these medals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:07.360
Anway today, Vint serves in a bunch&nbsp;
of advisory roles all over the place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:11.680
For example, he serves as a Commisioner for the&nbsp;
Broadband Commision for Digital Development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.560
Similarly, he serves on the board of advisors of&nbsp;
Scientists and Engineers for America. Vint also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.800
regularly gives interviews and speeches regarding&nbsp;
his thoughts about the future of the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.520
There’s plenty of these on YouTube if you’re&nbsp;
interested. Meanwhile, Bob has chosen to lead a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.560
much more subtle life. He doesn’t serve on a bunch&nbsp;
of boards and do interviews all the time. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.280
he does still work at his non-profits and is still&nbsp;
committed to making the internet as accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:40.400
as possible. And that’s what happened to the&nbsp;
internet’s founders. Did you know about Vint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:45.440
and Bob before this video? Comment that down&nbsp;
below. Also, drop a like if you Vint and Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.840
deserve more recognition. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:53.600
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
